# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Expanded ELI5 blocks for the Commodore 64 for Beginners course (one per chapter)."""


def E(pic, words, tryit, keep):
    return ("\n\n".join([pic, "Now the real words. " + words, "Try it. " + tryit, "If you remember one thing: " + keep]), "")


FEY = [
 E("Imagine a toy box with a little brain inside, plus helpers. One helper paints pictures, one sings, and two keep an eye on the buttons and the doors. The brain gives orders by writing numbers on sticky notes in special mailboxes, and each helper reads its own mailbox.",
   "The brain is the 6510 processor. The painter is the VIC-II video chip, the singer is the SID sound chip, and the watchers are the two CIA chips for the keyboard, joysticks and ports. The mailboxes are memory cells. When you switch on, the C64 starts BASIC and shows READY. A command without a line number runs straight away, which is called direct mode.",
   "In the first lab type print 6*7 and watch the answer appear. Then type poke 53280,0 and the border turns black.",
   "you talk to every part of the C64 by putting numbers into the right memory cells."),
 E("Imagine giving a friend a stack of numbered cards with instructions. They always do the cards in number order, card 10, then 20, then 30, even if you handed them over shuffled. You can swap a card by writing a new one with the same number.",
   "A program is a list of numbered lines. BASIC keeps them sorted, RUN starts at the lowest, LIST shows them and NEW erases them. GOTO jumps to another line; jumping back up makes a loop that runs until you press RUN/STOP. REM lines are notes that BASIC skips.",
   "Write three print lines numbered 10, 20 and 30, then type them in a different order and see that RUN still follows the numbers.",
   "the line number decides the order, so leave gaps of ten to slip new lines in."),
 E("Imagine a shelf of labelled boxes. Some boxes only take numbers, some only take whole numbers, and some only take words. If you try to put a word in a number box, the shelf complains.",
   "Variables are named boxes. A plain name like A holds any number, A% holds whole numbers, and A$ holds text. Putting text in a number box gives ?TYPE MISMATCH ERROR. INPUT asks the person at the keyboard for a value. In BASIC V2 only the first two letters of a name count, so SCORE and SCALE are the same box.",
   "Write 10 input \"name\";n$ and 20 print \"hi \";n$, run it, and type your name.",
   "the last character of the name tells you what kind of box it is: $ for words, % for whole numbers."),
 E("Imagine running laps around a track with a counter clicking each lap, and a coach at the gate who only lets you through if you show the right ticket.",
   "FOR and NEXT repeat a block with a counter, and STEP decides how much it changes each lap; STEP -1 counts down. IF and THEN are the coach: the rest of the line only runs when the test is true. Tests use =, <>, <, >, <= and >=, and can be combined with AND and OR.",
   "Count down from 10 to 1 with STEP -1 and print LIFTOFF at the end.",
   "FOR repeats, IF decides, and a false IF skips the whole rest of its line."),
 E("Imagine asking a helper to fetch something. You leave a note saying where you were, they go off, and when they come back they find your note and you carry on. And imagine a recipe card with a list of ingredients you read one at a time, and a row of numbered jars.",
   "GOSUB jumps to a subroutine and RETURN comes back to the line after it; END stops the program from falling into the helpers by accident. READ takes the next value from DATA lines. DIM makes an array, a row of numbered boxes. RND gives random numbers, and INT(RND(1)*6)+1 rolls a dice.",
   "Make a subroutine that draws a line of dashes and call it three times with GOSUB.",
   "GOSUB goes, RETURN returns, END guards."),
 E("Imagine a long street of 65536 mailboxes. Most hold your things. Some are magic: put a number in the border mailbox and the edge of the screen changes colour. POKE is posting a number; PEEK is peeking in.",
   "Memory has 65536 cells, each holding 0 to 255. POKE address,value writes a cell and PEEK(address) reads one. The memory map shows which addresses do what: the screen starts at 1024, free RAM at 49152, and the chips at 53248. 53280 is the border colour and 53281 the background, with 16 colours numbered 0 to 15.",
   "Loop through all 16 colours on the border with a FOR loop and a short wait.",
   "POKE puts and PEEK peeks; the chips are just special mailboxes."),
 E("Imagine the screen is a grid of 1000 little windows, 40 across and 25 down. Each window has a number, and next to it a paint pot that decides its colour. Put a letter number in a window and a colour in its pot and it lights up.",
   "Screen memory starts at 1024, and a cell's address is 1024 + row*40 + column. Colour RAM starts at 55296 with the same layout. POKE uses screen codes (A is 1) while PRINT uses PETSCII (A is 65). PRINT CHR$(147) clears the screen, and colour codes such as CHR$(28) switch the text colour.",
   "Put a star at row 12, column 20 by working out the address, and give it a colour.",
   "cell = 1024 + row times 40 + column, and always set the colour too."),
 E("Imagine a sticker that you can slide around a picture without smudging it. You draw it on a tiny grid of dots, tell the painter which page of your notebook it is on, where to put it, what colour it is, and then say go.",
   "A sprite is 24 by 21 dots, stored in 63 bytes, three per row. The data goes in a 64-byte block such as 832, and pointer 2040 holds the block number (13). With V=53248, V and V+1 are the position, V+39 the colour, and V+21 switches sprites on, one bit each. V+23 and V+29 make it double size.",
   "Read the ball DATA into 832, point 2040 at 13, set a position and colour, switch it on, and slide it across with a loop.",
   "data, pointer, place, paint, power on."),
 E("Imagine three singers. You tell them how loud the room is, how each note should swell and fade, how high to sing and with what kind of voice. Then you open the door and they start singing, and when you close it, they fade away.",
   "The SID starts at S=54272. S+24 is the volume, S+5 and S+6 are the envelope (attack, decay, sustain, release), S+1 and S are the pitch, and S+4 picks the waveform: 16 triangle, 32 sawtooth, 64 pulse, 128 noise. Adding 1 opens the gate and starts the note; writing it without the 1 lets the note fade.",
   "Play one note with volume 15, a triangle wave and the gate open, then close the gate after a wait.",
   "volume, envelope, pitch, then open the gate."),
 E("Imagine five light switches that are on when nobody touches them, and go off when you press them. To check one switch, you cover the others with your hand and look only at that one.",
   "A joystick is five switches: up 1, down 2, left 4, right 8 and fire 16. PEEK(56320) reads port 2 and PEEK(56321) port 1. A pressed switch reads 0, so you test it with IF (J AND 16)=0. GET reads a key without stopping the program; if no key is waiting it gives an empty string.",
   "Print FIRE! whenever fire is pressed, and use the pad under the screen to test it.",
   "pressed is zero, and AND picks out one switch."),
 E("Imagine a house with different doors: two small ones for game controllers, a big back door for building your own gadgets, a door for the tape player, a door for the disk drive, and a slot for cartridges. Each door is connected to one of the helpers inside.",
   "The control ports are 9-pin sockets: pins 1 to 4 are directions, 6 is fire, 7 is +5 V and 8 is ground. The user port is an edge of the circuit board with eight lines, PB0 to PB7: POKE 56579,255 makes them outputs and POKE 56577 sets them on or off. The serial port talks to disk drives and printers, the cassette port to the tape drive, and the expansion port takes cartridges. All lines are 5 volt logic signals.",
   "Make all user port lines outputs and run a single lit line from PB0 to PB7, doubling the value each time.",
   "each port is wired to a chip, so it is just a few memory cells, and it only likes gentle 5 volt signals."),
 E("Imagine a flip book. On each page you look at the controller, move the hero a little, check if anything happened, and draw the picture. Flip through fast and it moves.",
   "Every game is one loop: read the controls, move things, apply the rules, show the result, and go round again. The state lives in variables such as X for position and SC for score. A bounds check keeps the sprite on screen, and remembering whether fire was already down makes one press count once. Sound and flashes go in a subroutine.",
   "Add steering, then scoring, then a blip with a border flash, testing after each step.",
   "read, move, rules, show, and repeat."),
 E("Imagine talking to the brain in its own tiny language. It has three pockets, A, X and Y. You say: put 2 in pocket A, now put whatever is in pocket A into the border mailbox, now go back home.",
   "Machine code is the processor's own language, written in assembly as short words. LDA #2 loads the number 2 into A, STA $D020 stores A into the border cell, and RTS returns to BASIC. The $ means hexadecimal, so $D020 is 53280 and $C000 is 49152. The lab assembles your text, puts it at $C000 and starts it with SYS 49152.",
   "Make the border red and the background black in assembly.",
   "load, store, return, and don't forget the RTS."),
 E("Imagine putting stickers along a row of boxes. You keep a finger counting: stick one, move your finger along, check if you are at the end, and if not, go again. And imagine a toolbox full of ready-made tools you can borrow by calling their names.",
   "A loop in assembly uses X as the counter: STA $0400,X stores into the cell at $0400 plus X, INX adds one, CPX #40 compares, and BNE jumps back while they are not equal. The KERNAL is the C64's built-in toolbox; JSR $FFD2 calls CHROUT, which prints the character in A. A zero byte marks the end of a message.",
   "Fill the whole top row with hearts, then print a message with a loop that stops at the zero byte.",
   "count, compare, branch, and JSR to borrow the KERNAL's tools."),
 E("Imagine the picture on the screen is painted by a tiny brush that sweeps line by line, top to bottom, fifty times a second. If you change the paint while it is halfway down, only the bottom half changes. An interrupt is a tap on the shoulder when the brush reaches a line you chose.",
   "A PAL frame has 312 lines of 63 cycles. $D012 holds the line being drawn. Changing the border in a tight loop gives stripes; waiting for the line to change gives steady bars. A raster interrupt needs SEI, the handler address in $0314 and $0315, the raster enable in $D01A, a line in $D012, and CLI. The handler acknowledges in $D019 and ends with RTI.",
   "Make raster bars, then set up an interrupt that counts frames in a memory cell while BASIC keeps working.",
   "the screen is drawn line by line, and an interrupt lets you act at exactly the right moment."),
 E("Imagine writing a letter in friendly words and handing it to a translator, who rewrites the whole thing in the brain's tiny language before anyone reads it. The translated version runs very fast.",
   "C is a compiled language. cc65 compiles your C to assembly, ca65 assembles it and ld65 links it with the C library into a .prg file that the C64 runs. A program starts in int main(void). Variables need types; unsigned char (0 to 255) is fastest on the 6510. for loops count, == tests equality, and peekpoke.h gives POKE and PEEK.",
   "Print HELLO FROM C, then print the seven times table with a for loop.",
   "compile, assemble, link, and prefer unsigned char."),
 E("Imagine the helpers inside the C64 only read numbers in their mailboxes. They don't care if the note was written in BASIC, in assembly or in C. And imagine a cheat sheet of answers worked out in advance, so you don't have to do hard sums while the game is running.",
   "The registers are the same from every language: POKE(53269U, 1) switches a sprite on just like POKE 53269,1. (j & 8) == 0 tests the joystick. for (;;) loops forever, and waiting for raster line 250 moves things once per frame. A lookup table of precomputed numbers makes smooth curves without slow maths.",
   "Steer a sprite with the joystick from C, then make it swing by doubling the numbers in the wave table.",
   "the chips only see cells and numbers, so any language can drive them."),
 E("Imagine the C64 forgets everything when you turn it off, like a whiteboard wiped clean. To keep your work, you copy it onto a tape, like recording a song, or onto a disk, like a record with a list of songs in the middle.",
   "SAVE \"NAME\",8 saves to disk and LOAD \"NAME\",8 loads it back. LOAD \"$\",8 then LIST shows the directory. Device 1 is tape, 4 the printer and 8 the first disk drive. A 1541 disk has 35 tracks with the directory on track 18. Emulators keep disks and tapes as .d64, .tap and .prg files.",
   "Plan how you would save your game, check it with VERIFY, and list the disk to find it again.",
   "8 for disk, 1 for tape, and save before you switch off."),
]
